# Fuzz harness for tests/fuzz.c

## Context

`tests/fuzz.c` is currently a placeholder (`main` just returns 0) so `make`
can link `build/fuzz` before real tree logic existed. `src/rbtree.c` now has
a complete `rb_insert`/`rb_delete`/`rb_validate`/`rb_foreach` implementation,
and the Makefile already wires the fuzz binary into the test/asan/memcheck
targets:

- `make test` → `./build/fuzz 100000`
- `make memcheck` → `./build/fuzz 20000` under valgrind

So the harness needs to accept an op count from `argv[1]`, run randomized
`rb_insert`/`rb_find`/`rb_delete` against the real tree, cross-check every
result against an independent sorted-array oracle, and call `rb_validate`
at least every 100 ops. Per the user's review of earlier drafts, it also:
exercises long and adversarial keys (not just short alnum ones), routes its
own heap allocations through `rb_malloc`/`rb_free` (so a future change to
that allocator is automatically stress-tested by the fuzzer too), handles
`rb_insert`'s documented `-1` path instead of just asserting success, and
cross-checks `rb_foreach`'s in-order traversal against the oracle.

`build/fuzz` links `src/rbtree.c` + `src/rb_alloc.c` directly (see the
Makefile's `$(FUZZBIN)` rule), so `src/rb_alloc.h`'s declarations
(`rb_malloc`/`rb_free`) are available to link against; fuzz.c pulls them in
the same way `tests/test_rbtree.c` reaches into `src/` — a relative quoted
include (`#include "../src/rb_alloc.h"`), since `CFLAGS` only has
`-Iinclude`. Otherwise this stays a black-box harness against
`include/rbtree.h` only, matching the current placeholder.

## Design

**Oracle**: a sorted-by-key array of `{const char *key; int value;}`,
capacity `KEYSPACE` (fixed, since at most `KEYSPACE` distinct keys can ever
be live). `key` aliases into the key pool below rather than owning a copy.
Binary search (`strcmp`) for lookup; insert/delete shift elements to keep it
sorted.

**Key pool**: `KEYSPACE` (4096) strings generated once at startup via
`rb_malloc`, each iteration picking one at random by index. Length is drawn
50/50 from a short range (1–8 bytes) and a long range (9–500 bytes). Each
entry is also drawn from one of four categories so the pool mixes plain keys
with adversarial ones:
  - **normal**: random alphanumeric content.
  - **repeated-char**: one random byte repeated for the whole length, so two
    such keys of different length force `strcmp` to scan a long common
    prefix instead of short-circuiting early.
  - **prefix-of-previous**: built from `key_pool[i-1]` by either dropping
    its last byte or appending one — deliberately creates prefix/extension
    relationships between adjacent entries (this also incidentally produces
    the empty-string key when shrinking a length-1 key), which exercises
    the `lo`/`hi` open-bound comparisons in `rb_validate_node` and the
    ordering logic generally.
  - **raw-bytes**: content drawn from the full 1–255 byte range (never
    `\0`, since keys are NUL-terminated C strings), to catch any comparison
    code that implicitly assumes printable/7-bit content or signed-`char`
    semantics — `strcmp` must compare as `unsigned char` regardless.

